{"title":"2026 AMC 8 Regional Problems/Problem 24","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_24\">Problem 24</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AMC_8_Regional_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 24\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Charlotte the spider is walking along a web formed by the sides and diagonals of a square, as shown in the figure below. The web has <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> outer points and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> center point. Each time Charlotte reaches a point, she randomly chooses a neighboring point and moves to that point. Charlotte starts at one of the outer points and makes <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> moves (re-visiting points is allowed). What is the probability she is now at the center point?\n</p>\n<figure class=\"mw-halign-center\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:2026_AMC_8_Regional_Problems-Problem_24.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/8/86/2026_AMC_8_Regional_Problems-Problem_24.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"320\" height=\"303\" class=\"mw-file-element\" /></a><figcaption></figcaption></figure>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/7/a770b948c6348133ff903e73cae555e0d431b38e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A)}\\ \\frac{7}{27}\\qquad\\textbf{(B)}\\ \\frac{11}{36}\\qquad\\textbf{(C)}\\ \\frac{5}{18}\\qquad\\textbf{(D)}\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\qquad\\textbf{(E)}\\ \\frac{10}{27}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"407\" height=\"38\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AMC_8_Regional_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let the center point of the square web be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> and the four outer corner vertices be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/6/8/c68a167ae8ef8f2f20d9288a91dca1ffcbbc087d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O_1, O_2, O_3,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"87\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/4/a940a755d925b95bb8eda6037e39eaf142156f39.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O_4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"23\" height=\"17\" />. From any outer vertex, there are exactly <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> available paths because it connects to two adjacent outer vertices and the center point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />. From the center point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> available paths connecting to each of the outer vertices. Charlotte begins her walk at an outer point, say <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/2/1/3216e8846eb44f1055c322fb5bd30169d5d62cec.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" />. On her first move, she must choose one of its <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> neighbors, meaning she moves to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> with a probability of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/1/4e1965f8d70832ded66434c75a7bd52b75090af0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /> or to an adjacent outer point with a probability of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/4/7547747b133453d3c209cff6cfbf129aa67a6947.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{2}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" />. We track the paths that lead her to the center point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> on exactly her third move. One successful path scenario is when she visits the center on her first move, leaves it on her second move, and returns on her third move. The probability for this sequence is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/8/4/884f5e13a7c387693a171c7f2caa7d65ac5fce03.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{3} \\times 1 \\times \\frac{1}{3} = \\frac{1}{9}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"119\" height=\"38\" />, because from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> she is guaranteed to step to an outer point, and from that outer point she has a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/1/4e1965f8d70832ded66434c75a7bd52b75090af0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /> chance to return to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />. The other successful path scenario is when she stays on the outer boundary for her first two moves and steps to the center on her third move. The probability of stepping to a neighboring outer point on move one is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/4/7547747b133453d3c209cff6cfbf129aa67a6947.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{2}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" />. From that new outer point, she must step to another outer point on move two with a probability of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/4/7547747b133453d3c209cff6cfbf129aa67a6947.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{2}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" />. Finally, from that position, she steps to the center on move three with a probability of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/1/4e1965f8d70832ded66434c75a7bd52b75090af0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" />. The probability for this second scenario is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/f/eff03a5d7e45548576f5f9c2935051bb8817d808.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{2}{3} \\times \\frac{2}{3} \\times \\frac{1}{3} = \\frac{4}{27}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"132\" height=\"38\" />. Adding the probabilities of these two mutually exclusive cases together gives a total probability of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/5/e05bf9edb5114cdb5c87d9605b2a50c4bd7c795e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{9} + \\frac{4}{27} = \\frac{3}{27} + \\frac{4}{27} = \\frac{7}{27}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"197\" height=\"38\" />.\n</p><p>The correct answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/a/e/cae3ca350f6404c722984d13a579363c1eaff787.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(A)}\\ \\frac{7}{27}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"70\" height=\"49\" />.\n</p><p>~Icosahedron0288\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AMC_8_Regional_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AMC_8_Regional_Problems\" title=\"2026 AMC 8 Regional Problems\">2026 AMC 8 Regional Problems</a></li></ul>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017185255\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.019 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.031 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 144/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 0/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 2/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 5081/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    0.000      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:37405:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017185255 and revision id 291903. Rendering was triggered because: page-view\n -->\n</div>"}